          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            There is a very general notion of an
            %em injective object
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is equipped with more
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} structure and property
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+and+abelian+categories"} or a relative
            thereof.
          %p
            The concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolutions"} resolutions
            by injective objects –
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+resolutions"} injective resolutions
            – is crucial in the discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functors"} derived functors
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functors+in+homological+algebra"} derived functors in homological algebra
          %p
            Being injective is a
            %em property
            of an object; the corresponding
            %em structure
            is called an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+injective"} algebraic injective
          %h3#general_definition General definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi Mor
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J \subset Mor(C)
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              Frequently,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
              is the class of all
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphisms"} monomorphisms
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+monomorphisms"} regular monomorphisms
            %p
              When
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+chain+complexes"} category of chain complexes
              equipped with the injective
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+chain+complexes"} model structure on chain complexes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
              is often its class of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrations"} cofibrations
          #InjectiveObjects.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              An object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
