                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#preservation_of_injective_objects Preservation of injective objects
          #RightAdjointsOfExactFunctorsPreserveInjectives.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              Given a pair of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+functor"} additive
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi R
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟵
                        %mi L
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (L \dashv R)
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\mathcal{B}
                    \\stackrel{\overset{L}{\longleftarrow}}{\underset{R}{\longrightarrow}}
                    \\mathcal{A}
            %p
              between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+categories"} abelian categories
              such that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+exact+functor"} left exact functor
              (thus automatically exact), then the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
              preserves injective objects.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_5 Proof
            %p
              Observe that an object is injective precisely if the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
              into it sends
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphisms"} monomorphisms
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphisms"} epimorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              preserves monomorphisms by assumption of (left-)exactness. With this, the statement follows via the adjunction isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi ℬ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Hom_{\mathcal{A}}(-,R(I))\simeq Hom_{\mathcal{B}}(L(-),I)
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              Additivity of the left adjoint follows from the remaining assumptions, since
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+functor#SufficientConditions"} exact functors preserve biproducts
          %p The preceding lemma has the following variant:
          #Adjuncts_Injectives.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_9 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
              be categories and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi R
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L\dashv R:\mathcal{D}\to\mathcal{C}
              be an adjunction. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              maps monos to monos, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              maps injectives to injectives.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_6 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I\in\mathcal{D}
              be injective. Consider the following diagram in
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ↣
                              %mi m
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi R
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi I
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    A &amp; \overset{m}{\rightarrowtail} &amp; B
                    \\\
                    f\downarrow &amp;    &amp;
                    \\\
                    R(I) &amp;   &amp;
                    }
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta: Hom_\mathcal{C}(X,R(Y))\overset{\simeq}{\rightarrow}Hom_\mathcal{D}(L(X),Y)
              the natural bijection given by the adjunction. Consider now the following diagram in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
              where the assumptions ensure that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(m)
              is monic:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi A
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ↣
                              %mrow
                                %mi L
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi m
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi B
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi θ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi I
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    L(A) &amp; \overset{L(m)}{\rightarrowtail} &amp; L(B)
                    \\\
                    \\theta(f)\downarrow &amp;    &amp;
                    \\\
                    I &amp;   &amp;
                    }
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              is injective, there there exists a filler
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta(g):L(B)\to I
              which by the adjunction must come from a uniquely determined
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi I
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:B\to R(I)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mover
                          %mo ↣
                          %mrow
                            %mi L
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi m
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi L
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mrow
                            %mi θ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi I
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mover
                          %mo ↣
                          %mi m
                        %mi B
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mi g
                        %mi R
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi I
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\frac{L(A)\overset{L(m)}{\rightarrowtail} L(B)\overset{\theta(g)}{\rightarrow}I}{A\overset{m}{\rightarrowtail} B\overset{g}{\rightarrow}R(I)}
            %p
              correspond to each other under the bijection, whence we have
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta(g\circ m)=\theta(g)\circ L(m)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta(g)\circ L(m)=\theta(f)=\theta(g\circ m)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
              is a bijection, it follows that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo =
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f=g\circ m
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(I)
              is injective.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_5 Remark
            %p
              The proof transposes the proof of the dual statement 10.2. in (
              %a{:href => "#HiltonStammbach71"}> Hilton-Stammbach 1971
              , p. 82): In situation
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L\dashv R
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              maps epis to epis, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              maps projectives to projectives.
          #Exponential_injectives.num_remark
            %h6#remark_6 Remark
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in\mathcal{C}
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                      %mo −
                    %mo ×
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}_-\times:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{C}
              exists and has a right adjoint
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mrow
                        %mo −
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mo ⊣
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo −
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}_-\times X\dashv (_-)^X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                      %mo −
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}_-\times X
              preserves monos, it follows that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (_-)^X
              preserves injectives.
            %p
              In particular, for toposes, this implies that all power objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^X
              are injective, since the injectivity of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              follows more or less straightforwardly from its classifying properties.
          %h3#ExistenceOfEnoughInjectives Existence of enough injectives
          %p
            We discuss a list of classes of categories that have
            %em enough injectives
            according to def.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EnoughInjectives"}
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_10 Proposition
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              has enough injectives.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_7 Proof
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+object"} power object
              can be shown to be injective (cf. the above
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "#Exponentials_injectives"} remark
          #AbHasEnoughInjectives.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_11 Proposition
            %p
              Assuming some form of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
              has enough injectives.
          %p
            Full AC is much more than required, however;
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+violations+of+choice"} small violations of choice
            suffice.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_8 Proof
            %p
              By Proposition
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InjectiveAbelianGroupIsDivisibleGroup"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
              is an injective
              = succeed "-module" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/divisible+group"} divisible group
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
              of a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/divisible+group"} divisible group
            %p
              To start with, notice that the group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
              is divisible, and hence the canonical embedding
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{Q}
              shows that the additive group of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
              embeds into an injective
              = succeed "-module." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            %p
              Now, by the discussion at
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+module"} projective module
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              admits an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi S
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\oplus_{s \in S} \mathbb{Z}) \to A
              from a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+group"} free
              abelian group, and hence is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+group"} quotient group
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
              of copies of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde A
              of the direct sum of copies of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℚ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ker
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mi ker
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mo ⊕
                              %mrow
                                %mi s
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi S
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↪
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mo ⊕
                              %mrow
                                %mi s
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi S
                            %mi ℚ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↪
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mi A
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    ker &amp;\stackrel{=}{\to}&amp; ker
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    (\oplus_{s \in S} \mathbb{Z}) &amp;\hookrightarrow&amp; (\oplus_{s \in S} \mathbb{Q})
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    A &amp;\hookrightarrow&amp; \tilde A
                    }
            %p
              Here,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde A
              is divisible because the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
              of divisible groups is again divisible, and also the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+group"} quotient group
              of a divisible groups is again divisble. So this exhibits an embedding of any
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              into a divisible abelian group, hence into an injective
              = succeed "-module." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
          #TransferOfEnoughInjectivesAlongAdjunctions.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_2 Lemma
            %p
              Given a pair of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+functor"} additive
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi R
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟵
                        %mi L
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (L \dashv R)
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\mathcal{B}
                    \\stackrel{\overset{L}{\longleftarrow}}{\underset{R}{\longrightarrow}}
                    \\mathcal{A}
            %p
              between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+categories"} abelian categories
              such that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+functor"} exact functor
                  and
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful functor
            %p
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
              has enough injectives, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              also has enough injectives.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_9 Proof
            %p
              Consider
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathcal{A}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
              has enough injectives, there is an injective object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \in \mathcal{B}
              and a monomorphism
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \colon L(A) \hookrightarrow I
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunct"} adjunct
              of this is a morphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\tilde i \colon A \longrightarrow R(I),
            %p and so it is sufficient to show that
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi I
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(I)
                  is injective in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒜
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde i
                  is a monomorphism.
            %p
              The first point is the statement of Lemma
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RightAdjointsOfExactFunctorsPreserveInjectives"}
            %p
              For the second point, consider the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde i
              as part of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequence"} exact sequence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ker
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mover
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    ker(\tilde i)\longrightarrow A \stackrel{\tilde i}{\longrightarrow} R(I)
                    \\,.
            %p
              By the assumption that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+functor"} exact functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is still exact:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ker
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mover
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    L(ker(\tilde i)) \longrightarrow L(A) \stackrel{L(\tilde i)}{\longrightarrow} L(R(I))
                    \\,.
            %p
              Now observe that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(\tilde i)
              is a monomorphism. This is because its composite
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mover
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi ϵ
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(A) \stackrel{L(\tilde i)}{\longrightarrow} L(R(I)) \stackrel{\epsilon}{\longrightarrow} I
              with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction+unit"} adjunction unit
              is (by the formula for
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjuncts"} adjuncts
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ker
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(ker(\tilde i)) \to L(A)
              is the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+morphism"} zero morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ker
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ker(\tilde i) \to A
              is zero, and hence that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ker
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mover
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ker(\tilde i) = 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde i
              is a monomorphism.
          #RModHasEnoughInjectives.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_12 Proposition
            %p
              As soon as the category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
              has enough injectives, so does the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
              over some
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %p
              In particular, if the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
              holds, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Mod
              has enough injectives.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_10 Proof
            %p
              Observe that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi R
                    %mi Mod
                    %mo →
                    %mi AbGp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\colon R Mod \to AbGp
              has both a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_!
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+scalars"} extension of scalars
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              to
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_*
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coextension+of+scalars"}> coextension of scalars
              ). Since it has a left adjoint, it is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+functor"} exact
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TransferOfEnoughInjectivesAlongAdjunctions"}
              from prop.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AbHasEnoughInjectives"}
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_13 Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo =
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R = k
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
              \=
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective
          #AbelianSheavesHaveEnoughProjectives.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_14 Proposition
            %p
              The category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaves"} abelian sheaves
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ab
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab(Sh(C))
              on any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+site"} small site
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
              over
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            A proof of can be found in
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"} Peter Johnstone
            = succeed "," do
              %em Topos Theory
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_7 Remark
            %p
              This is in stark contrast to the situation for
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+objects"} projective objects
          .num_cor
            %h6#corollary_3 Corollary
            %p
              The category of sheaves of modules over any
              %span.newWikiWord
                sheaf of rings
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/sheaf+of+rings"} ?
              on any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+site"} small site
              also enough injectives.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_11 Proof
            %p
              Combining prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AbelianSheavesHaveEnoughProjectives"}
              with prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AbHasEnoughInjectives"}
              (which relativizes to any topos).
          %p
            This slick proof of this important fact was pointed out by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Colin+McLarty"} Colin McLarty
            in an email to the categories list dated 10 Oct 2010.
          %h3#InjectiveResolutions Injective resolutions
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_15 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              be an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in \mathcal{A}
              there is an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+resolution"} injective resolution
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mo •
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    J^\bullet = [J^0 \to \cdots \to J^n \to \cdots] \in Ch_(\mathcal{A})
            %p
              equipped with a a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+complexes"} cochain complexes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ∼
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \stackrel{\sim}{\to} J^\bullet
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi ⋯
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi ⋯
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi J
                              %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi J
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi ⋯
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi J
                              %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi ⋯
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X &amp;\to&amp; 0 &amp;\to&amp; \cdots &amp;\to&amp; 0 &amp;\to&amp; \cdots
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    J^0 &amp;\to&amp; J^1 &amp;\to&amp; \cdots &amp;\to&amp; J^n &amp;\to&amp; \cdots
                    }
                    \\,.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"}> projective object
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+presentation"} projective presentation
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+cover"} projective cover
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+resolution"} projective resolution
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+module"} projective module
            %li
              %p
                %strong> injective object
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+presentation"} injective presentation
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+envelope"} injective envelope
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+resolution"} injective resolution
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+module"} injective module
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraically+injective+object"} algebraically injective object
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+object"}> free object
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+resolution"} free resolution
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+module"} free module
            %li
              %p
                flat object,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+resolution"} flat resolution
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+module"} flat module
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonality"} orthogonality
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+modules"} injective modules
            was introduced in
          %ul
            %li#Baer
              R. Baer,
              %em Abelian groups that are direct summands of every containing abelian group
              , Bulletin AMS
              %strong 46
              no. 10 (1940) pp.800-806. (
              %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.bams/1183503234"}> projecteuclid
              )
          %p
            The dual notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+modules"} projective modules
            was considered explicitly only much later in
          %ul
            %li#CartanEilenberg56
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henri+Cartan"}> Henri Cartan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Eilenberg"} Samuel Eilenberg
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homological+Algebra"} Homological Algebra
              %strong 19
              (1999) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691049915/homological-algebra-pms-19-volume-19"}> ISBN:9780691049915
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1515/9781400883844"} doi:10.1515/9781400883844
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~mmovshev/BOOKS/homologicalalgeb033541mbp.pdf"} pdf
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li#HiltonStammbach71
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Hilton"}> Peter Hilton
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Stammbach"} Urs Stammbach
              = succeed "," do
                %em A course in homological algebra
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4419-8566-8"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4419-8566-8
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://web.math.rochester.edu/people/faculty/doug/otherpapers/hilton-stammbach.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            (used, e.g., for the proof of
            = succeed ");" do
              %a{:href => "#Adjuncts_Injectives"} Lemma
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Masaki+Kashiwara"}> Masaki Kashiwara
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Schapira"} Pierre Schapira
              = succeed "_" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+and+Sheaves"} Categories and Sheaves
          %p
            Using tools from the theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+categories"} accessible categories
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jiri+Rosicky"}> Jiri Rosicky
              ,
              %em Injectivity and accessible categories
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.muni.cz/~rosicky/papers/acc5.ps"}> ps
              )
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Baer"} Baer’s criterion
            is discussed in many texts, for example
          %ul
            %li
              N. Jacobsen,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Basic Algebra II
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              T.-Y. Lam,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lectures on modules and rings
          %p
            For injective objects in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            see
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                ,
                %em Handbook of Categorical Algebra vol. 3
                , Cambridge UP 1994. (section 5.6, pp.314-315)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+Mac+Lane"}> Saunders Mac Lane
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} Ieke Moerdijk
                %em Sheaves in geometry and Logic
                , Springer Heidelberg 1994. (section IV.10, pp.210-213)
            %li
              %p
                D. Higgs,
                %em Injectivity in the topos of complete Heyting algebra valued sets
                , Can. J. Math.
                %strong 36
                (1984) pp.550-568. (
                %a{:href => "http://cms.math.ca/openaccess/cjm/v36/cjm1984v36.0550-0568.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Linton"} Fred Linton
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Par%C3%A9"} Robert Paré
                %em Injective Objects in Topoi II: Connections with the axiom of choice
                , pp.207-216 in LNM
                %strong 719
                Springer Heidelberg 1979.
            %li
              %p
                T. Kenney,
                %em Injective Power Objects and the Axiom of Choice
                , JPAA
                %strong 215
                (2011) pp.131–144.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Linton"}> Fred Linton
                ,
                %em Injective Objects in Topoi III: Stability under coproducts
                , Bull. Acad. Polon. Sci. Sér. Sci. Math. Astronom. Phys.
                %strong 29
                (1981) pp.341-347.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Linton"}> Fred Linton
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Par%C3%A9"} Robert Paré
                %em Injective Objects in Topoi I: Representing coalgebras as algebras
                , pp.196-206 in LNM
                %strong 719
                Springer Heidelberg 1979.
          %p
            Discussion of injective objects (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"}> types
            ) in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %ul
            %li#Escardo19
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mart%C3%ADn+Escard%C3%B3"}> Martín Escardó
              ,
              %em Injectives types in univalent mathematics
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.01211"}> arXiv:1903.01211
              )
          %p For a detailed discussion of internal notions of injectivity see
          %ul
            %li#Harting
              Roswitha Harting,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Locally injective abelian groups in a topos
          %p
            For injective toposes in the 2-category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+topos"} bounded toposes
            see
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                %em Injective Toposes
                , pp.284-297 in LNM
                %strong 871
                Springer Heidelberg 1981.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                %em Sketches of an Elephant vol. 2
                , Cambridge UP 2002. (section C4.3, pp.738-745)
